/* icache_footprint: count the distinct lines ever filled into the icache
   (ICACHE_UNIQUE_FILL_LINES). Measurement only -- one hash lookup per fill,
   no effect on timing. */
DEF_PARAM(icache_footprint, ICACHE_FOOTPRINT, Flag, Flag, TRUE, )

/* --ifetch_xtion_stats: a TRANSITION MATRIX over per-line instruction-fetch outcomes.
 *
 * For every on-path instruction fetch, classify the outcome into one of five categories, look up
 * what the PREVIOUS fetch of that same line was classified as, and charge the matrix square
 * [previous][current]. The question it answers is not "how often does each outcome happen" --
 * ICACHE_MISS_NOT_PREFETCHED and friends already say that -- but "what does a line's history
 * predict about its next fetch": does a line that FDIP missed once keep being missed, or does the
 * prefetcher pick it up next time round.
 *
 * PURELY DESCRIPTIVE. The five categories are a partition of fetch outcomes and the ordering
 * below is declaration order, nothing else. No category is treated as better or worse than any
 * other anywhere in the implementation, and the chain carries no weighting.
 *
 * THE FIVE CATEGORIES, and exactly what lands in each:
 *   TIMELY  the line was resident in the L1I when the fetch arrived. Includes a --perfect_icache
 *           synthesized hit, which has no line behind it, and includes a hit on a line a previous
 *           DEMAND fetch installed -- residency is what is observed here, not provenance.
 *   DMISS   the fetch missed the L1I and NO request for the line was in flight, so the fetch
 *           itself had to go out. This is get_miss_reason returning IMISS_NOT_PREFETCHED (FDIP
 *           never produced the line) or IMISS_TOO_EARLY_EVICTED_BY_{IFETCH,FDIP} (it did, and the
 *           line was evicted before use). Both are "no request in flight", which is the defining
 *           property; read the existing ICACHE_MISS_* chains to split them.
 *   MSHORT  the fetch missed and merged into an in-flight prefetch whose in-flight time at the
 *           merge was BELOW the edge.
 *   MLONG   the same, with in-flight time AT OR ABOVE the edge.
 *           The edge is --ifetch_xtion_merge_edge, defaulting to L1_CYCLES, and the measured
 *           quantity is cycle_count - req->emitted_cycle. emitted_cycle rather than
 *           fdip_emitted_cycle for the reason mem_req_match_reqbuf documents: the latter is set
 *           only for the two FDIP types, while MRT_IPRF is an instruction prefetch a demand can
 *           merge into just the same.
 *   OTHER   a request for the line WAS in flight, but it is not attributable to a live prefetch --
 *           get_miss_reason returned a non-MSHR-hit reason while a request existed. That happens
 *           when the fetch merged into another DEMAND fetch's request, and when the prefetch that
 *           covers the line was issued before FDIP's last recovery cycle (get_miss_reason treats
 *           such a prefetch as not having happened). A real and bounded case, not a grab bag.
 *
 * ON-PATH ONLY. An off-path fetch is recorded nowhere and does not advance the clock below. A
 * wrong-path fetch of a line says nothing about what the line's next on-path fetch will be, and
 * letting it overwrite the stored category would make every square downstream of a mispredict
 * describe speculation rather than the program.
 *
 * NOT CACHE-INDEXED. The previous category lives in its own open hash table keyed on line
 * address, never in Cache_Entry, so a line that is evicted and refetched keeps its history -- the
 * whole point is to follow a line ACROSS evictions. The table is unbounded, like
 * icache_footprint_lines, and sized by --ifetch_xtion_buckets -- whose default is one bucket per
 * line of the largest footprint these traces reach, so lookups stay O(1) in practice.
 *
 * REUSE DISTANCE PER SQUARE, IN MLC FILLS. Each square also accumulates the distance between the
 * two fetches it connects, measured in INSERTIONS INTO THE MLC -- allocation opportunities at the
 * L2, counting the ordinary fill path, writeback fills and --instr_upfill demotions alike, since
 * all three consume a way. IFETCH_XTION_RD_<from>_<to> is the SUM and its ratio column is the
 * mean distance for that square.
 *
 * WHY THAT UNIT: it makes a mean distance directly comparable against MLC CAPACITY
 * (--mlc_cache_assoc x sets). A reuse inside capacity could have been served from the MLC if
 * nothing displaced the line; one outside it could not have been, whatever the policy. A distance
 * in fetches or cycles answers no such question. 0 is a real value -- no MLC fill intervened, so
 * nothing could have displaced the line -- the same convention --load_prio_stats uses.
 *
 * The clock is NOT ROI-gated, so it does not equal MLC_FILL + MLC_WB_FILL in the stats (those
 * are); the relationship holds inside the target window only. And because demotions advance it,
 * the unit itself shifts between an --instr_upfill arm and a baseline: compare distances against
 * capacity within an arm, and be careful comparing them across arms.
 *
 * Do NOT compare these against the --reuse_dist_stats or --load_prio_stats chains: those count
 * per-SET accesses and per-SET misses, on different clocks with different populations.
 *
 * FIRST TOUCH. A line with no stored history has no predecessor and no distance, so it is charged
 * to IFETCH_XTION_FIRST_<current> instead and contributes to no square. sum(squares) +
 * sum(FIRST_*) is every recorded on-path fetch, which is the closure check.
 *
 * WINDOW: target-only, on the early_evict_in_roi() gate, like every other counter in this family.
 * The per-line history and the clock are NOT gated -- a line first fetched during warmup must
 * still carry a category into the target window, or every early transition reads as FIRST. */
DEF_PARAM(ifetch_xtion_stats, IFETCH_XTION_STATS, Flag, Flag, FALSE, )
/* 0 means "use L1_CYCLES", so the edge tracks the configured LLC latency unless pinned. */
DEF_PARAM(ifetch_xtion_merge_edge, IFETCH_XTION_MERGE_EDGE, uns, uns, 0, )
/* Sized for one entry per bucket at the largest instruction footprint these traces reach.
   Measured with NORESET_ICACHE_UNIQUE_FILL_LINES on the google set: 43k (arizona) to 186,438
   (sierra.a.3) distinct instruction lines per run, so 2^18 leaves the worst workload at 0.71
   entries per bucket and costs 2MB of bucket array. Raise it for a larger footprint -- the table
   never evicts, so a run that touches more lines than this just chains. A power of two is
   expected: HASH_INDEX derives its fold width from the bucket count. */
DEF_PARAM(ifetch_xtion_buckets, IFETCH_XTION_BUCKETS, uns, uns, 262144, )

/* REPL_SRRIP_T (--mlc_cache_repl_policy 24): SRRIP with a per-access-type insertion value and a
 * per-access-type PROMOTION VECTOR, over 3 bits of metadata (RRPV 0..7, 7 distant).
 *
 * Standard SRRIP has one insertion RRPV and promotes every hit to 0. This generalises both ends:
 *
 *   INSERTION. --srrip_t_insert is five DIGITS, one per FILL class, in enum order:
 *       pref-no-merge, pref-MLONG, pref-MSHORT, demand-instruction, other
 *     A fill of class c installs at that RRPV. 7 means "evict me first", 0 means "most protected".
 *
 *   PROMOTION. --srrip_t_pv_<class> is eight DIGITS, one per CURRENT RRPV, for
 *     each HIT class. An access of class t that hits a line whose RRPV is i sets that line's RRPV
 *     to T_t[i]. The vector is chosen by the type of the ACCESS DOING THE HITTING, not by what
 *     filled the line -- a line filled by a prefetch and later hit by a demand fetch is promoted
 *     by the demand's vector. Plain SRRIP is the all-zeros vector 00000000; the identity vector
 *     01234567 means "this access does not change the line's position at all".
 *
 * THE TWO TAXONOMIES DIFFER, which is not an oversight. See Srrip_T_Fill and Srrip_T_Hit in
 * cache_lib.h: a demand-instruction MISS cannot be a hit, and the MLONG/MSHORT lead-time split
 * describes the instant a demand caught a prefetch in flight, which says nothing about an access
 * that found the line resident. The hit side instead needs the demand-instruction HIT, which the
 * fill side has no equivalent of. So there are five insertion values and four promotion vectors.
 *
 * THE MLONG/MSHORT EDGE is --srrip_t_merge_edge, 0 meaning L1_CYCLES. The measured quantity is
 * Mem_Req.instr_merge_delta -- cycles the prefetch had been in flight when the first demand
 * ifetch merged into it -- which is maintained unconditionally, so this policy does not require
 * --fdip_delta_place or any other arm. Same quantity and same default edge as
 * --ifetch_xtion_stats, so the policy and that matrix bucket identically.
 *
 * DEFAULTS REPRODUCE PLAIN SRRIP at 3 bits: every insertion 6 (one below distant) and every
 * promotion vector all-zeros. A run that selects policy 24 and sets nothing is therefore a clean
 * 3-bit SRRIP baseline, which is the control this policy should be read against -- NOT against
 * --mlc_cache_repl_policy 16, whose RRPV is 2-bit and whose distant value is 4.
 *
 * WRITE THEM AS BARE DIGIT STRINGS WITH NO QUOTES: --srrip_t_insert 66666. A descriptor's
 * "params" string is expanded UNQUOTED by scarab-infra ($SCARABPARAMS in
 * run_memtrace_single_simpoint.sh), so quote characters survive into argv and a quoted value
 * fails to parse, while an embedded space splits the argument in two. Every value is a single
 * digit, so no separator is needed at all. Commas, spaces and stray quotes ARE skipped by the
 * parser, so an already-written descriptor keeps working -- but the bare form is the one that
 * cannot go wrong.
 *
 * VALIDATED AT INIT. Every digit must lie in 0..7 and each string must have exactly its expected
 * length; anything else is fatal at startup rather than silently truncated. A value above 7 would
 * leave the victim scan's aging loop unable to ever find a distant line. */
DEF_PARAM(srrip_t_insert, SRRIP_T_INSERT, char*, string, "66666", )
DEF_PARAM(srrip_t_pv_pref_unmerged, SRRIP_T_PV_PREF_UNMERGED, char*, string, "00000000", )
DEF_PARAM(srrip_t_pv_pref_merged, SRRIP_T_PV_PREF_MERGED, char*, string, "00000000", )
DEF_PARAM(srrip_t_pv_demand_instr, SRRIP_T_PV_DEMAND_INSTR, char*, string, "00000000", )
DEF_PARAM(srrip_t_pv_other, SRRIP_T_PV_OTHER, char*, string, "00000000", )
DEF_PARAM(srrip_t_merge_edge, SRRIP_T_MERGE_EDGE, uns, uns, 0, )

/* REPL_SRRIP_FE (--mlc_cache_repl_policy 25): SRRIP keyed on the FE-BOUND MARK, over 3 bits of
 * RRPV (0..7, 7 distant).
 *
 * Where REPL_SRRIP_T classifies a fill by its merge structure, this one classifies it by whether
 * the existing front-end-bound policy MARKED it -- the same is_fe_bound that --mlp_lin_instr_lambda
 * consumes, i.e. an instruction fill whose FE-bound fraction cleared the threshold.
 *
 *   INSERTION. --srrip_fe_insert is four DIGITS, one per fill class, in enum order:
 *       marked-demand, marked-merge, other-instruction, other
 *     "marked-demand" is a demand instruction fetch (MRT_IFETCH) that was marked. "marked-merge"
 *     is an instruction prefetch a demand merged into that was marked, which requires
 *     --mlp_lin_instr_include_pref_merge or the class is always empty.
 *     "other-instruction" is every REMAINING instruction fill -- unmerged FDIP prefetches (the
 *     bulk of the instruction stream), fills whose FE-bound fraction missed the threshold, and
 *     merges the lead-time gate rejected. Keyed on mem_req_type_is_instr, deliberately NOT on
 *     the classifier's `instr_fill`: that flag means "eligible for the FE window", which an
 *     unmerged prefetch never is, so keying on it would hide ~21.75M instruction fills per the
 *     measured MLC mix inside the data class.
 *     "other" is then data traffic only: loads, stores, data prefetch, writebacks.
 *
 *     The first three classes together are exactly mem_req_type_is_instr, which is also exactly
 *     the population --srrip_fe_pv_instr promotes -- so the insertion and promotion vectors cut
 *     traffic on the same instruction/data boundary.
 *
 *   PROMOTION. --srrip_fe_pv_instr and --srrip_fe_pv_other are eight DIGITS each, one per CURRENT
 *     RRPV. An access of class t hitting a line at RRPV i sets it to T_t[i]. The vector is chosen
 *     by the ACCESS doing the hitting: instruction traffic of any kind (demand ifetch or an
 *     FDIP/IPRF prefetch, merged or not) takes _instr, everything else takes _other.
 *     Only two vectors because a hit has no mark of its own -- the FE-bound classification happens
 *     at a FILL, so there is nothing to key a finer split on without reading the line's fill-time
 *     mark, which would contradict "chosen by the access".
 *     All-zeros is plain SRRIP; the identity vector 01234567 leaves the line where it is.
 *
 * THE MARK MUST BE LIVE. membound_classify_fill returns early when no stats knob and no lambda
 * asks for a classification, which would leave is_fe_bound permanently FALSE and silently collapse
 * classes 0 and 1 into nothing. Selecting this policy self-opens it, so no extra knob is needed --
 * but --mlp_lin_instr_thresh / --td_fe_rrip_thresh still set the threshold the population is cut
 * at, and on these traces that population is ~1% of MLC fills. Read MLC_FEBOUND_FILL before
 * concluding anything from an insertion sweep.
 *
 * WRITE THEM AS BARE DIGIT STRINGS WITH NO QUOTES, for the reason the --srrip_t_* block gives.
 * Commas, spaces and stray quotes are skipped by the parser; the bare form cannot go wrong.
 *
 * DEFAULTS REPRODUCE PLAIN SRRIP at 3 bits: every insertion 6 (hence four 6s), every promotion
 * vector all-zeros.
 * Read this policy against policy 25 with its defaults, not against policy 16, whose RRPV is
 * 2-bit with distant 4. */
DEF_PARAM(srrip_fe_insert, SRRIP_FE_INSERT, char*, string, "6666", )
DEF_PARAM(srrip_fe_pv_instr, SRRIP_FE_PV_INSTR, char*, string, "00000000", )
DEF_PARAM(srrip_fe_pv_other, SRRIP_FE_PV_OTHER, char*, string, "00000000", )

DEF_PARAM(icache_read_ports, ICACHE_READ_PORTS, uns, uns, 1, )
DEF_PARAM(icache_write_ports, ICACHE_WRITE_PORTS, uns, uns, 1, )
DEF_PARAM(icache_banks, ICACHE_BANKS, uns, uns, 8, )
DEF_PARAM(icache_repl, ICACHE_REPL, uns, uns, 0, )

